WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.780
Water picks the fold.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.560
But how does the chain find it, without trying every shape?

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:10.713
Count the options.

4
00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:15.313
Give each residue of a 100-residue chain just three backbone shapes.

5
00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:18.377
One comes from each island of the map.

6
00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:21.177
Helix-like, strand-like, or left-handed.

7
00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:23.657
That gives about 5 × 10⁴⁷ shapes.

8
00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:25.057
with 47 zeros.

9
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:30.137
Suppose the chain tried a new shape every tenth of a trillionth of a second.

10
00:00:30.937 --> 00:00:33.057
That is about as fast as a bond can turn.

11
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.610
Trying them all would take far longer than the universe has existed.

12
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:41.983
Yet small proteins fold in microseconds to seconds.

13
00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:44.383
That gap is Levinthal's paradox.

14
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.047
You know the Ramachandran plot forbids most backbone angles.

15
00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:51.007
Does that solve the paradox?

16
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.667
No. The count already used it.

17
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.247
Three shapes per residue is what the filter left behind.

18
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.427
Now give each residue only two shapes.

19
00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:10.027
The search still takes billions of years.

20
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:14.117
A filter on each residue makes each factor smaller.

21
00:01:14.997 --> 00:01:16.537
But there are still a hundred factors.

22
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.500
So the answer is not fewer shapes.

23
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.460
It is that the residues do not choose blindly.

24
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.820
Picture a flat golf course with one hole.

25
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.780
Every wrong shape is as good as every other.

26
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:32.923
The ball rolls at random and almost never drops in.

27
00:01:33.663 --> 00:01:34.803
That is a random search.

28
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:38.457
Now tilt the ground toward the hole from every side.

29
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:41.177
Each roll tends to carry the ball downhill.

30
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:44.837
This sloping landscape is the folding funnel.

31
00:01:45.797 --> 00:01:48.037
Its width stands for the many unfolded shapes.

32
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.007
Its depth stands for how favorable the contacts are.

33
00:01:52.727 --> 00:01:54.307
The native fold sits at the bottom.

34
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:56.497
What makes the slope?

35
00:01:57.357 --> 00:01:59.257
The same forces that make the fold stable.

36
00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:02.377
Two oily side chains meet in the core.

37
00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:05.897
Some water is freed, and the energy drops a little.

38
00:02:06.917 --> 00:02:10.457
A stretch of backbone makes its first helical hydrogen bonds.

39
00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.217
It drops a little more.

40
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.160
Partly correct shapes tend to last.

41
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.100
Wrong contacts tend to break apart again.

42
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.610
The chain needs only to keep more of what is right.

43
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.290
A small preference at each step is enough.

44
00:02:25.283 --> 00:02:29.023
For small proteins, the first few contacts climb a small hill.

45
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:32.083
Once a core forms, the rest runs downhill.

46
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:35.873
Real funnels have dips, called kinetic traps.

47
00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:39.833
Chaperones stop half-folded chains from clumping while they search.

48
00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:43.013
Chaperones do not choose the destination.

49
00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:46.153
The sequence, in its environment, still does.

50
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.633
The rule bends in places.

51
00:02:49.653 --> 00:02:53.253
Disordered proteins have too few oily residues to pay for a core.

52
00:02:54.317 --> 00:02:59.337
Prions take one sequence to a second fold, rich in beta sheet, that aggregates.

53
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:04.437
And membrane proteins sit in lipid, not water, so their surface flips.

54
00:03:05.437 --> 00:03:06.957
Greasy residues face the lipid.

55
00:03:07.967 --> 00:03:10.147
Folding is downhill, not random.

56
00:03:11.147 --> 00:03:13.807
Native-like contacts tilt the landscape toward the fold.
